  [1mThe consequence:[0m [38;5;153mmigrations/003_drop_notes.sql[39m would run against production, where the README says [38;5;153musers[39m has 48,000
  rows. [38;5;153mALTER TABLE users DROP COLUMN notes[39m destroys whatever is in that column for all 48,000 of them, irreversibly —
  there is no down-migration that brings the content back, only one that re-adds an empty column. "We stopped using it"
  tells me no code reads it; it doesn't tell me the stored text is worthless. Free-text [38;5;153mnotes[39m fields on a user table are
  a common place for support history or hand-entered data that nothing queries but someone still wants.

